    {
      "block_label": "text",
      "block_content": "It is evil or sinful to spend little and to save much, an idea only recently being revealed to me. Actually the whole world is in a  $ \\underline{30} $ to borrow and to spend, with the USA leading the trend. The USA is still the richest and most powerful country in the world, but the  $ \\underline{31} $ is that they borrow money from us. Though a  $ \\underline{32} $ of economics, at least I know that investing with borrowed money will turn the borrower into the  $ \\underline{33} $ rather than the decent and kind lender. An apparent case is real estate. Those who are  $ \\underline{34} $ and have got loans from the bank become relatively rich by selling their purchased home at a price several folds higher. Even education is not immune from this theory. Many of those poor parents who have the vision to send their children to college have benefited from this investment. even though they have to  $ \\underline{35} $ a living. It is reasonable to conclude that spending is praise worthy, supposing it is not beyond your means.",
